Recognizing the importance of enhancing recognition and enforcement of arbitral awards in electronic form and use of notices of arbitration in electronic form to promote greater legal clarity and broader acceptance of legal documents in arbitration,
Expressing its appreciation to Governments and relevant international intergovernmental and non-governmental organizations for their contributions to the work of Working Group II (Dispute Settlement), and the Working Group for formulating these texts,
1.	Adopts the revised articles of the UNCITRAL Model Law on International Commercial Arbitration (1985), as amended in 2006, together with corresponding revisions to the accompanying Explanatory Note, as they appear in annex IV to the report of the United Nations Commission on International Trade Law on the work of its fifth-ninth session;
2.	Recommends that all States give favourable consideration to the UNCITRAL Model Law on International Commercial Arbitration, as amended in 2006 and 2026, when they enact or revise their laws, in view of the desirability of uniformity of the law of dispute settlement procedures and the specific needs of international commercial arbitration practice;

3.	Adopts the guidance text on the form of awards as it appears in annex V to the report of the United Nations Commission on International Trade Law on the work of its fifth-ninth session, for inclusion as additional note in the UNCITRAL Notes on Organizing Arbitral Proceedings adopted in 2016 (with an additional note on early dismissal and preliminary determination adopted in 2023 and an additional note on form awards adopted in 2026);
4.	Recommends the use of the UNCITRAL Notes on Organizing Arbitral Proceedings adopted in 2016 (with an additional note on early dismissal and preliminary determination adopted in 2023 and an additional note on form awards adopted in 2026) by parties to arbitration, arbitral tribunals and administering institutions in the settlement of disputes arising in the context of international commercial relations;
5.	Requests the Secretary-General to publish the above-mentioned texts including electronically, and in the six official languages of the United Nations, and to make all efforts to ensure their wide dissemination, so as to enhance their visibility and accessibility and facilitate their use by all relevant stakeholders.”
